00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.749 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  75 years ago this weekend saw the end of  britain's colonial rule of india the  nation of 340 million people was divided  creating two independent countries  muslim majority pakistan and hindu  majority india it's a moment in history  known as partition it triggered a  massive forced migration and left an  estimated two million people dead in  religious violence mts tayab looks at  how the trauma of the event still  affects south asians generations later  an unprecedented mass migration has  begun as millions of people make their  way across borders the consequence of a  century-long british strategy of divide  and rule never has the marvel cinematic  universe introduced viewers to a moment  in history quite like this  and transported one of its superheroes  on a journey of self-discovery to the  site of one of the largest and deadliest  mass migrations in modern human history  but that's exactly what the disney plus  limited series ms marvel does iman  valani plays gamalakhan a muslim  pakistani american teenager from new  jersey  who gains superpowers from a mysterious  bengal that once belonged to her  grandmother who disappeared during the  partition of british india 75 years ago  fatima oscar wrote the acclaimed episode  which tackles the painful history  head-on  why does what happened  in 1947 still resonate  so strongly with so many south asians  that when creating ms marvel the series  it became such a  big story arc there's so much silence  that has been around partition right  there's silence that the west has had  around partition but there's also  silence in south asian communities and  the south asian diaspora around  partition in part it's because it's such  a traumatic  um event we've become increasingly more  aware  trauma lives in the body and it's passed  down through generations and so  we can feel some of this pain even if we  don't have words for it generational  trauma is central to ms marvel's origin  story like in this poignant scene  between kamala and her grandmother  portrayed by legendary pakistani film  actress samina ahmed  i'm still trying to figure out who i am  my passport is pakistani my roots are in  india  and in between all of this  there is a border  there is a border marked with blood and  pain  those borders were drawn in just five  weeks by a british civil servant who had  never traveled further east than paris  or understood india's religious  linguistic and cultural diversity after  two centuries of brutal colonial rule  britain's exit from the colony it  plundered such a vast fortune from  quickly turned into a bloodbath fleeing  from their looted bloodstained towns  comes a new exodus it's estimated up to  two million people were killed and  around 20 million were displaced as they  tried to make their way to what became  muslim majority east and west pakistan  or hindu majority india  by 1858  the crown ruled all of india a painful  past other south asian creatives want  people to better understand  at london's british film institute is  child of empire an animated virtual  reality journey through partition which  follows the stories of two characters  making opposite journeys across the new  borders  irfan sadati and sparshahuja created the  project partition was 75 years ago  why  does this story matter today  we still talk about the holocaust the  world war ii or the armenian genocide as  things that continue to be to be part of  lived world history  i think partition in the same manner is  the defining moment of modern south asia  the other reason why we don't want to  leave partition in the past is that the  united kingdom tends to see partition as  south asian history when it is  very much british history  in the three quarters of a century since  independence tensions between india and  pakistan have boiled over into four wars  a nuclear arms race and the creation of  bangladesh in 1972 as a new generation  tries to reckon with the pain of  partition through art fatima oscar hopes  it will lead to a more unified future  when you look at an event like partition  and you see even 75 years later that  things like that are happening today  you're like this is important like this  is why we need to actually break some of  these silences to contend with our  history to deal with our pain and to  create these lines of solidarity so that  we can actually protect each other do  you know what you are for cbs saturday  morning in kyoto london i'm a superhero  wow so important well it's still  stunning to hear the numbers two million  people estimated to have died yes 20  million  displaced in the partition incredible  number and as she said you keep seeing  it happen over and over in history  and people don't know about it which is  why it's so important like pieces like  this to be done so we know it is a part  of your past it's a part of your future  generations

